- Malaria outbreak reported at Seva Shram School in Rayagada, Odisha, with the Badaraising Ashram School in Jemadeipenta Panchayat identified in reporting. A fifth-grade girl, Khushi Kilaka, died during malaria treatment after returning to the hostel from a 10-day break; fever developed soon after. Blood tests at Jemadeipenta Health Center confirmed malaria, and she was referred to the Rayagada District Headquarters Hospital where she died. Her younger brother also tested malaria-positive and is hospitalized under ongoing care. Health authorities have launched an investigation, screening other students, and implementing vector-control measures on campus, including cleaning and insecticide spraying, to prevent mosquito-borne diseases in ashram schools. Published 11 November 2026."} ,{
- Malaria confirmed as cause of death in Rayagada, Odisha, after a Class V girl, Khusi Kilaka, at Badaraising Ashram School in Jemadeipentha Panchayat, Rayagada Sadar block, developed fever and tested malaria positive. She returned to the hostel after a 10-day leave, was treated with anti-malaria drugs following a blood test, but her condition deteriorated on 10 November 2026 and she died during treatment at the District Headquarters Hospital. Her younger brother also tested positive for malaria and is undergoing treatment at a hospital. The incident underscores ongoing malaria concerns in the Rayagada area; no source of infection nor outbreak details are provided beyond the two affected siblings.
- Malaria death at Badaraising Ashram School, Jemadeipentha Panchayat, Rayagada district, Odisha: Class 5 student Khusi Kilaka died after malaria was confirmed by blood test and she received anti-malarial treatment; she died while hospitalized at District Headquarters Hospital, Rayagada. Her younger brother, also a resident student at the same school, tested positive for malaria and is in critical condition, admitted to hospital. The siblings had returned after a 10-day vacation. The incident underscores concerns about malaria transmission among students in residential tribal institutions and highlights the need for timely diagnosis, adequate healthcare facilities, and preventive measures in such settings. Laboratory confirmation by blood test supports the diagnosis, intensifying calls for p
